    "title": "Where did Artemis 2 land? Artemis returns to Earth after 10-day mission",
    "text": "Updated April 10, 2026, 6:51 p.m. MTThe Orion spacecraft has splashed down in the Pacific Ocean southwest of San Diego, ending Artemis II’s historic 10-day mission to the moon and back. Following liftoff on April 1, the 10‑day mission sent four astronauts on a flyby around the moon, testing life‑support, navigation and propulsion systems in deep space without landing, NASA said.The mission has now concluded, splashing down off the coast of California near San Diego at around 6:07 p.m. MT/7:07 p.m. CT.How long was the Artemis 2 mission?It was a 10‑day mission that sent four astronauts on a flyby around the moon. It launched on April 1.Where did Artemis 2 land?The Orion spacecraft splashed down in the Pacific Ocean off the coast of San Diego.How big is the Artemis capsule?About 16.5 feet wide (about 5 meters in diameter). Orion’s interior space is about the size of two minivans combined, making it significantly roomier than the Apollo-era capsules.Artemis III launch dateNASA's next mission under the Artemis program is slated to launch sometime in 2027.Preparations for Artemis III are already underway before the Artemis II mission is even back on the ground. NASA is preparing to roll out and transport to Florida a new core stage for the gigantic 322-foot Space Launch System rocket that would help launch the mission.What did Artemis 2 do?Artemis II astronauts tested NASA’s Orion spacecraft during a crewed mission in deep space. They checked major systems such as life support, propulsion, power and navigation while practicing manual flying, long‑distance communications and other critical mission tasks.The crew also conducted limited science work and observed the Moon. The mission ended with Orion’s re‑entry and splashdown, helping confirm the spacecraft is ready for future Moon missions.Artemis 2 splashdown videoNASA has a live stream providing continuous coverage of the Artemis II mission.USATODAY contributed to this report.",
